:: The reason to do a true, random-assignment experiment to control for exactly the type of problems in interpretation that [[User:Gnangarra|Gnan]] and [[User:Vexations|Vexations]] have raised. Editors' motivations and availability to participate in Wikipedia vary between people and over time within the same person.  The assumption in a random assignment experiment is that the people who receive the experimental treatment (i.e., a barnstar or a thanks) are on average the same as those who are in the non-treatment, control condition.  Therefore, if the treatment and control groups differ on some outcomes of interest, such as the amount and type of work they do on Wikipedia, their willingness to talk on article talk pages, etc.) one can attribute these differences to the experimental treatment.  The standard inferential statistics, like t-tests and regression analyses, gives an estimate about how likely the observed difference would occur by chance because of natural variations between people and time. [[User:Robertekraut|Robertekraut]] ([[User talk:Robertekraut|talk]]) 15:57, 28 December 2018 (UTC)
